<h2>A Games-era dispute, decided a generation later</h2>
<p>The underlying contract belongs to the frantic build-up to the 2010 Commonwealth Games: CPWD\'s composite tender for IAAF-approved synthetic athletic tracks at five venues, with the respondent contractor executing the Jawaharlal Nehru Stadium work under an agreement of October 2009. After a High-Level Committee reviewing Games expenditure found the rates inflated, the final bill was settled as a "Zero Bill" with Rs. 3.20 crore withheld. The contractor took its claims to a sole arbitrator, who allowed several of them in July 2015 — and the Union\'s Section 34 petition then waited over a decade in the Delhi High Court\'s commercial list before being decided this week.</p>

<h2>The disclosure question under the old law</h2>
<p>Before the 2015 amendment, Section 12 required an arbitrator to disclose circumstances likely to give rise to justifiable doubts about independence or impartiality, and permitted challenge on that ground — but contained none of the present Schedules that make specified relationships disqualifying per se. The Court\'s analysis proceeded in three steps.</p>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Characterise the prior role</strong> — the arbitrator sat on a committee examining quoted rates at the tender stage; that exercise ended with the tender and had no nexus with the contract\'s performance or the disputes that later arose.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Test for justifiable doubts</strong> — mere prior professional contact with the subject-matter, without involvement in the disputes, did not meet the threshold of Section 12(3) as it then stood.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Apply the knowledge bar</strong> — under Section 12(4), a party may challenge an arbitrator it appointed only for reasons it becomes aware of afterwards; the Union, which constituted the very committee, could not plead late discovery. The omission to disclose, the Court held, is not an independent ground of challenge.</div>
</div>

<h2>Waiver and conduct</h2>
<p>The Union\'s partial compliance with the award — payment of interest and compliance with another direction — was argued by the contractor as acceptance. The Court rejected waiver on those facts, but decided the petition against the Union on the merits of each ground instead. The sequence is a useful reminder that paying under an award while challenging it does not necessarily waive the challenge, though parties should record reservations when making such payments.</p>

<h2>Claim-by-claim: why the merits grounds failed</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Claim</th><th>Union\'s objection</th><th>Court\'s answer</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rs. 2.35 crore deducted for FOP consultant visits</td><td>Deduction justified by negotiation letter</td><td>The letter did not require seven separate visits; deduction without prior notice unjustified</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reduced rates for excess quantities</td><td>Rates properly reduced</td><td>No notice under Clause 12.3 — the contractual precondition — was given</td></tr>
<tr><td>Discus cage deduction</td><td>Work deficient</td><td>No contractual basis for the deduction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delay damages</td><td>Beyond the reference</td><td>The Union itself relied on the contractor\'s undertaking, bringing the issue within the reference</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The transitional lesson</h2>
<p>A surprising number of pre-2015 arbitrations are still working their way through Section 34 and Section 37 dockets. For those matters, counsel must resist arguing the modern Schedules by reflex: eligibility and disclosure are tested under the unamended Section 12, where the question is always whether justifiable doubts actually arise on the facts, and where a party\'s own prior knowledge can be fatal to the challenge. Conversely, for arbitrations commenced after 23 October 2015, the same facts might produce a different result — which is precisely why identifying the applicable regime is the first step in any bias-based challenge.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The petition was dismissed and the award of 9 July 2015 upheld; pending applications were disposed of. The decision turns on the unamended Act and the specific tender-stage character of the arbitrator\'s earlier role.</p>
</div>
